I’ll inspect the specimen metadata, diffraction pattern, and the frozen analysis instructions first. Then I’ll identify candidate phases, build/refine the complete GSAS-II model, run the required one-time bounded polish, and export all deliverables from the accepted project.

The sample is a CuO + WO₃ reaction fired at 700 °C, so CuWO₄ is the principal chemical expectation, but I’m treating that only as a hypothesis: residual CuO/WO₃ and off-stoichiometric Cu–W–O phases remain plausible. I’m now extracting the peak families and checking the exact frozen COD index and GSAS-II workflow requirements before committing phases.

Peak-family mapping now supports two ordinary phases, not just the expected tungstate: triclinic CuWO₄ explains the dense main family, while cubic Cu₃WO₆ independently matches the otherwise unexplained peaks at approximately 15.18, 17.64, 19.79, 21.75, 32.49, and 33.77°. A separate family near 22.67, 26.07, 28.14/28.49, 32.78, and 33.09° remains, so I’m testing WO₃ polymorphs and other physically balanced companions rather than treating profile mismatch as a new phase.

